[637,3,0,1906,"Newsboys at the Brooklyn Bridge","A group of newsboys stand in the shadow of the Brooklyn Bridge with stacks of newspapers. Hine's pictures showed that many boys worked long hours selling papers in city streets.","What time of day do you think they started work?"],
[642,4,0,1906,"San Francisco After the Earthquake","On April 18, 1906, a huge earthquake and the fires that followed destroyed much of San Francisco and killed about three thousand people. This view of the ruins shows block after block flattened.","What would it be like to see your city like this?"],
[644,0,0,1907,"Hollow Horn Bear","Hollow Horn Bear was a Lakota leader who served as a scout and a chief. Edward Curtis photographed him in 1907, and his face later appeared on a US postage stamp.","What can you tell about him from his face?"],
[646,3,0,1907,"The Steerage","On a ship crossing to Europe, Alfred Stieglitz looked down from the first-class deck at passengers in the cheaper steerage section. He rushed to get his camera and had one plate left. The shapes of the gangway, funnel and straw hat make a picture that helped begin modern photography.","Find the straight lines and the round shapes. How do they fit together?"],
[647,0,0,1907,"Frank Eugene Seated at a Table","Alfred Stieglitz made some of the first color photographs in America with Autochrome plates. Here his friend Frank Eugene, a photographer and painter, sits at a table with flowers and glasses.","Do the soft colors feel like a painting?"],
[648,0,0,1907,"Child with Striped Dress","A girl in a straw hat and striped dress leans sideways in the sun in this Autochrome by Stieglitz. The glowing pink and blue come from tiny grains of colored starch.","How is it different from a black-and-white portrait?"],
[653,3,0,1908,"Sadie Pfeifer, Cotton Mill Spinner","Sadie Pfeifer, a young girl, stands at a spinning frame in a South Carolina cotton mill. Lewis Hine compared children's heights with his coat buttons to estimate how old they were. His photographs helped end child labor.","How would you feel working in a mill instead of going to school?"],
[654,3,0,1908,"A Young Trapper Boy","In coal mines, trapper boys sat alone in the dark and opened and closed doors to let cars through. They sometimes worked ten hours a day. Hine photographed boys like this one in West Virginia.","How would you spend ten hours in the dark?"],
[663,0,0,1908,"Leo Tolstoy in Color","Sergey Prokudin-Gorsky took this color portrait of the famous writer Leo Tolstoy sitting in a garden at his estate in 1908. It is one of the first color photographs of a major writer.","What does it feel like to see someone from over a century ago in color?"],
[664,0,0,1908,"Mark Twain in Color","Mark Twain, the author of Tom Sawyer, liked to write in bed. This Autochrome shows him in a red robe, reading with white hair against the pillow.","What book would you read in bed?"],
[665,3,0,1908,"Glass Factory Workers","Boys who worked in an Indiana glassworks pose in front of the factory. Lewis Hine photographed child workers and kept notes of their names and ages, hoping to show that laws were needed.","How old do you think the youngest boy is?"],
[666,3,0,1908,"Midnight at the Glassworks","Boys work through the night in a glass factory, where a furnace glows in the dark. Hine sometimes sneaked into factories pretending to be a salesman, so he could photograph the working children.","Why do you think the boys worked at night?"],
[667,8,0,1908,"Spiderwebs","Alvin Langdon Coburn photographed the tall rigging of a ship reflected in the water so it forms a web of lines. He wanted pictures to be as beautiful as paintings and prints.","Can you find the ship's masts and their reflections?"],
